rvane priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably in Q3 2026.\n- It costs more. Utility bond spreads widened from 130bp in Q1 to 135bp in Q2 and then to 165bp in Q3, which is 30bp in one quarter. That raises the cost of the 2027 plans but doesn't show the market is closed.\n- Debt alone probably can't cover Pellham's whole gap. Only Norvane's bond is documented, so I can't say whether Pellham could raise the debt it needs.\n\n**Equity markets (n6)**\n- Equity is the weak spot. Pellham's C$2.4B share offering was pulled on 14 October. Two other utility offerings were cut back in size in Q3.\n- The credit note describes equity markets as \"less receptive\".\n- Pellham is the largest utility buyer of grid equipment in Caldria. It says it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and resize its Q1 2027 tender (previously about C$900M). The children read this as capex being scaled back because funding is tight. That is their inference, not something Pellham stated.\n\n**Gaps**\n- Neither child found the 2027 capex totals, the utilities' leverage or credit ratings, or their planned debt issuance.\n- The equity conclusion rests on one pulled deal and a qualitative market note.\n- The equity child did not read the tender calendar or the Pellham market statement, which hold the 2027 capex amounts.\n\n**Implication for grid-equipment demand:** capex that depends on equity is likely to be cut or deferred, as Pellham's order cut shows. Norvane-type issuers can likely keep funding through bonds.","ttft_ms":1626,"type":"result","duration_ms":46063,"uuid":"a4bbc975-be48-424c-823c-0288d73b94d9","ttft_stream_ms":1179,"time_to_request_ms":381,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":16,"system_prompt":53,"resume_other":1,"process_user_input":2,"input_attachments":71,"input_hooks":12,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":35,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":44,"init_frame_send":6,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":26,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":4,"autocompact":2,"request_announcem